      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The comparison between carbon-ceramic and semi-metallic brake composites is quite a bit like comparing a high-end, well-tuned kitchen to a bustling, chaotic market square; the former is designed for precision, while the latter is built for volume and durability. During high-frequency, high-load deceleration cycles, the carbon-ceramic composite acts much like a disciplined, stoic librarian who keeps their cool under pressure, whereas the semi-metallic composite is more like a rowdy, enthusiastic crowd that gets hot and bothered quickly.\n\nWhen it comes to thermal conductivity, carbon-ceramic materials generally possess a lower thermal conductivity compared to the highly conductive graphite and metal fibers found in semi-metallics. This means that while carbon-ceramics might not spread heat away as rapidly as the semi-metallics, they excel in thermal mass and specific heat capacity. Think of the carbon-ceramic as a thick, heavy wool blanket that absorbs a lot of heat before its temperature rises significantly, much like a large, slow-moving river that doesn't warm up instantly under the sun. Conversely, the semi-metallic composite is like a thin sheet of aluminum foil; it heats up fast, dissipates heat quickly, but loses that heat",
      "source": "target",
